{
  "summary": "The three prior proposals converge on a reasonable decomposition but collectively suffer from a critical sequencing blind spot and two underexamined risks. First, on sequencing: all proposals treat modal extraction as the 'safest first step,' but this is a trap — modals are the least painful part of the monolith (they're already self-contained classes with clear boundaries at lines 568-1300), and extracting them first creates a false sense of progress while deferring the genuinely hard work. The actual first step should be extracting the ~70 _log_line call sites and the AgentStatus state management into a dedicated logging/status service, because this cross-cutting concern is the single thread that prevents clean separation of PipelineController from PipelineApp — every async worker method (_run_plan_async, _run_implement_async, _run_ideating_async) uses app.call_from_thread(self._log_line, ...) as its primary communication channel back to the UI, creating an implicit coupling that none of the proposals adequately address. Second, the third proposal's suggestion of using Textual's message system for the PipelineController's event-emitter interface sounds clean but ignores a practical constraint: the @work-decorated methods run in background threads and use app.call_from_thread() to marshal calls to the UI thread — Textual's post_message() is also thread-safe, but the current code relies on sequential execution within call_from_thread callbacks to maintain log ordering and update flags atomically (e.g., setting _plan_running = False and then calling _check_for_changes). Switching to an event-emitter pattern would require careful ordering guarantees that Textual's message queue doesn't inherently provide, risking subtle race conditions in agent status display. Third, none of the proposals address the elephant in the room: the STAGE_ACTIONS dictionary and get_contextual_bindings() method (lines 1734-1780) already implement a primitive declarative action dispatch, yet the action_* methods themselves contain redundant stage-checking guard clauses that duplicate this logic — the refactor should unify these into a single dispatch table with stage predicates rather than creating a new ActionDispatcher abstraction alongside the existing one, which would just add another layer of indirection without removing the duplication."
}
